00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.500
If that describes you, welcome.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.210
You're in the right place.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:42.830
There are evangelists
people who are

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:47.510
trying to get RStudio
and R more widely adopted

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:48.500
in their organizations.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.490
IT ops, of course, always happy
to have of IT ops on board.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:54.268
And anyone who
wants to try RStudio

00:01:54.268 --> 00:01:55.310
of Professional Products.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.560
If you don't know anything
about Professional Products

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:58.952
and this is your
first time here,

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:01.160
this is a great way to get
introduced to Professional

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.360
Products, we're going
to like actually

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:03.402
walk through all of them.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.240
So RStudio empowers
individuals to be

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.390
productive with data science.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:12.890
We believe in things like--

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.040
well, we're strong believers
in open source and reproducible

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:16.640
research.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.600
And we believe in things like
APIs and interoperability.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.540
We want to have our
work be really usable

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.070
with clear documentation.

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:28.370
We believe in inclusivity
and collective success.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.290
We really want all
of the work that we

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:31.700
do to have lasting value.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.090
So we take a long-term
approach here at RStudio.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:37.730
We build open source and
professional software

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.080
for data science.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.870
Our open source software is used
in almost every organization

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:45.920
that uses R. When organizations
benefit from open source

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.380
software, we think
they have an obligation

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.312
to reinvest in the
community that provided it.

00:02:51.312 --> 00:02:53.270
Organizations can contribute
to our open source

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:57.010
software in part by purchasing
our professional products.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.930
But our business does
not rely on altruism.

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:02.060
R professional features
include important things

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.030
that customers want like
security, authentication, load

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:07.150
balancing, and support
many other things.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:09.360
And these are the
products that we sell,

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.360
RStudio Server Pro, RStudio
Connect and RStudio Package

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:12.860
Manager.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:14.420
And then the money
that we make from

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.330
these professional products
funds are open source software.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.190
Most of the money
that we earn actually

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:22.603
goes into open source software.

00:03:22.603 --> 00:03:24.520
What's the relationship
between R and RStudio?

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.270
Well, we don't own R.
We don't package it.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:27.840
We don't distribute it.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.520
Our core team has 20 members.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.160
And zero of them
are from RStudio.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.740
We like to say RStudio
sits on top of R.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.660
So you standardize on R first.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.420
And then install
our products second.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.370
We assume that you have chosen
to invest in R. In fact,

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:45.595
last year, I gave a webinar
called Professional R

00:03:45.595 --> 00:03:47.970
Tooling and Integration, where
I talked about legitimacy,

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:49.440
competencies, and adoption.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.510
So legitimacy is
about recognizing R

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.022
as an analytic standard.

00:03:53.022 --> 00:03:55.230
And competencies are about
understanding and managing

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:56.490
R tooling.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:04:00.390
And adoption is about relying
on integrated R-based solutions.

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:03.330
So today, we're going to be
talking about competencies

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.610
and where those
competencies come from.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:06.750
How do you learn about R?

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.480
This is a big problem actually
with a lot of our customers.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.440
They don't know how to
administer our products

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.890
in their organization.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:16.890
And there's a couple
of reasons why.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:20.430
First of all, R is relatively
unknown in most organizations.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:23.070
There's no single place to get
all the information you need.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.270
It's really hard to see
the forest for the trees.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:26.820
There's a lot of
pieces involved.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.808
A lot of things are
changing very quickly.

00:04:28.808 --> 00:04:31.350
So we see a lot of our customers
going through a lot of trial

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:32.160
and error.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.830
Some of those include
organizational hurdles.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.027
For example, the
IT department might

00:04:37.027 --> 00:04:39.360
be completely separate from
the data science department.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.260
And maybe the communication
or the support isn't there.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.060
Or there might be
resource limitations.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.840
Like maybe there's the
funding for the headcount

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:51.540
is separate from the
funding for the servers

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:52.980
or the infrastructure.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:59.590
And people aren't able to get
the servers that they need.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:02.140
So today, I want to share some
best practices for managing

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:03.340
RStudio in production.

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:05.620
I want to share the
product requirements,

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:08.710
go over some tips, and give
you a path for getting started.

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:10.210
My goal is to give
you a big picture

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:12.790
view of what success
looks like, assuming

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:14.080
you're using R products.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:23.060
So I want to share five best
practices for administering

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:27.510
RStudio in production.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:30.750
Number 1, keep your
system up to date.

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:33.720
So you're going to want modern
tools for your data science

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:34.740
platform.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.800
That means modern operating
systems and modern browsers.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.450
You'll be using the
cutting edge tools.

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:41.033
You'll be doing
cutting edge analysis.

00:05:41.033 --> 00:05:44.970
You'll be hiring people
with very important skills.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.570
And you're going to want modern
tools for all of those things.

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:52.530
You'll need a C++ 11 compiler,
so that you can build the R

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:54.273
packages on your system.

00:05:54.273 --> 00:05:56.440
And you'll want to think
about your internet access.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.140
And that's because
all of your packages

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:01.810
are probably coming
from the internet.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:04.530
If you don't have internet
access in your server

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.610
environment-- and a lot
of organizations don't--

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:08.370
then it's very important
that you talk to RStudio,

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.037
because all of our
products are designed

00:06:10.037 --> 00:06:13.870
to run in an offline mode.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:17.457
Number 2, support
multiple versions of R?

00:06:17.457 --> 00:06:19.540
Why do you want to support
multiple versions of R?

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:22.020
Well, because you want to
be able to manage upgrades.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.850
You want to be able to test
code on a variety of R versions.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:26.590
And you want to
support projects that

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:31.600
depend on various versions
of R. All of our products

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:36.130
support multiple versions of R.
We recommend that you upgrade

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:38.440
at least yearly and that you
use a modern version of R,

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.050
so 3.1 or greater.

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:42.820
And then finally,
this is very important

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:45.400
that we recommend that
you build R from source.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.140
Building R from
source allows you

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.870
to install multiple
versions of R side by side.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:50.890
It's not hard to do.

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:54.100
It follows the standard
config, make install path.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:58.390
Most organizations have a
process for building software

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:00.307
from source already in place.

00:07:00.307 --> 00:07:02.140
And we have instructions
for how to do that.

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:08.720
Number 3, organize
your R packages.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.790
So R packages rule the nest.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:13.550
Packages will drive your
R version, your Linux

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:16.670
dependencies, and even
your operating system.

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:18.680
Data scientists will
want to access their most

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:19.940
beloved packages.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:22.370
So say you have a data scientist
who wants like the most

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:25.730
recent modeling package,
and they try to install it

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:28.850
and it fails because it
needs a new version of R.

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:30.662
So you want to upgrade
that version of R.

00:07:30.662 --> 00:07:32.120
And then you find
that you're going

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:33.627
to need some Linux binaries.

00:07:33.627 --> 00:07:35.210
But those Linux
binaries aren't really

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:36.440
supported by your
operating system.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:38.390
So suddenly you're upgrading
your whole operating system

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:40.340
to support that package
of R. That is not

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:42.110
an unrealistic scenario.

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:44.400
That's one reason we say
keep your platform modern

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:45.930
and up to date.

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:50.270
So it turns out that managing
packages for a single user

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:52.700
is quite easy, like
you do on your desktop.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:55.975
But managing packages for an
entire platform is really hard.

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:57.800
And we should
know, RStudio knows

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.300
a lot about managing packages.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.160
So we created a package manager.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:04.200
It's called the RStudio
Package Manager.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:06.490
And it solves several problems.

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:08.880
One problem is when your
environment is disconnected

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.150
from the internet or you're
in an air gapped environment.

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:14.490
You need a way to get packages
into that environment.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.393
And Package Manager
can help with that.

00:08:16.393 --> 00:08:18.810
Another one is being able to
serve consistent environments

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.470
to docker containers.

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:24.997
So if your docker container
is running one environment,

00:08:24.997 --> 00:08:26.580
it's probably not
the same environment

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:28.580
that you're building on,
but the Package Manager

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:31.500
can help standardize
those environments.

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:36.299
There's also security control
for package repositories.

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:38.970
A lot of organizations
like to curate

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:43.590
the packages that gets
delivered to their organization.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:45.120
And then also
Package Manager helps

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.460
with sharing internal packages.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:49.950
So since Package
Manager is brand new,

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:52.560
I thought I'd run through a
quick demo of how it works.

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.420
This is RStudio Package Manager.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:01.340
And it has this nice UI.

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:05.210
So people who can log
in and see all the

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:07.640
packages that are
available to them.

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:09.760
And in this case,
these are my packages.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:11.330
This is my description.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:15.450
I can see how often my packages
are being updated here.

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:21.500
So in this case, these
packages were updated March 17.

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:23.270
179 packages were added.

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:25.460
And 159 packages were archived.

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:28.475
And you can see that alpha sim
R package here was archived

00:09:28.475 --> 00:09:34.740
in version 0.9, because it
was upgraded to version 0.10.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.830
Now, all the packages
in this snapshot

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:40.700
are indexed with this
identifier, 1 3 0 3.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:44.270
If I want to see all
of the snapshots,

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:45.770
I can look at this
calendar function

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:49.610
and see the history of
when my package is updated.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:52.160
So my most recent
version was March 17.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.780
And here, I can see this is
the specific snapshot, 1303,

00:09:56.780 --> 00:10:00.380
that indexes that
specific set of packages.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.690
I can copy that URL.

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:04.940
And I can paste it into my IDE.

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:07.460
And that means that
all the packages

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:09.830
I pull from then
on in the IDE are

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:13.900
going to pull from a very
specific set of packages.

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:21.740
Then I can take that URL, the
package repository location,

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:23.922
and put that in my
docker container

00:10:23.922 --> 00:10:25.130
when I spin up the container.

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:28.370
And it will pull the same
packages I use for my analysis.

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:30.890
We call this a shared
baseline, so that everyone

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:34.340
is going to be using
the same packages that

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:37.350
are pegged to this checkpoint.

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:41.640
In addition to
indexing the packages,

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.290
I can also organize
multiple repositories.

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:46.800
So here, you see I have a
repository that consists just

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:50.580
of validated packages
for my group.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:53.130
This repository
has packages that

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:56.340
were contributed internally
from my organization.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:59.760
And this repository has packages
that are being pulled down

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:00.900
from GitHub.

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:05.190
So what Package
Manager allows me to do

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:06.960
is pull down
packages from GitHub

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:09.570
and compile them or
build them locally,

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:12.450
so that I can have the
most up to date packages.

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:18.670
I can also see the usage stats
for my packages, which is nice.

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:20.545
I can see which packages
are being downloaded

00:11:20.545 --> 00:11:23.990
and which licenses
are being used.

00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:26.870
So Package Manager is really
valuable to organizations

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:29.090
who are standardizing on
R. We hope you'll give that

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:30.778
a consideration.

00:11:30.778 --> 00:11:33.070
We'll be coming back and
visiting that in a little bit.

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:43.330
All right, so number 4,
use route privileges.

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:45.030
So the group in
your organization

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:48.150
that installs, configure,
and manages R and RStudio

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:50.320
will need route privileges.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:56.820
So RStudio installs require
privileges in RStudio products,

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:00.150
also run as root.

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:02.460
RStudio Server Pro
runs as the root user

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:05.850
in order to create a new R
session on behalf of its users.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:08.160
And RStudio Connect
runs as a user

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.110
in order to isolate
applications and processes.

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:16.470
I'll also add that system-wide
installations of R on Linux

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:20.550
oftentimes involve root as
well so this is very important

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:24.090
that the people that have the
permissions in your system

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:32.550
to install R are able
to do so effectively.

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:34.980
So that means if
that person is you,

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:38.430
then you need to know how
to install the products

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:42.450
and the software
sufficiently on that system.

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:44.580
And if that person
is someone else,

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:47.340
then you need to help
that person understand

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:48.900
what this process involves.

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:53.310
That means installing R, the
R packages, and the products.

00:12:53.310 --> 00:12:56.310
So the root user privileges,
you may or may not have that.

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:58.170
But whoever does have
that needs to have

00:12:58.170 --> 00:12:59.970
that understanding
of what it means

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:03.530
to support a platform on R.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:07.215
OK, and the last one, 5 is
securely manage your users.

00:13:07.215 --> 00:13:08.340
There's two types of users.

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:10.510
There's R programmers
and there's end users.

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:12.570
And the R programmers
will need access

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:15.090
to R, file shares,
databases, and probably

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:17.940
many other sensitive systems.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:21.540
Our processes for R
programmers run as the user

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:23.400
under a local account.

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:27.210
So users need local
accounts on the system

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:31.060
to be running these
types of analysis.

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:33.870
End users, on the
other hand, are

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:36.540
people that may or may not
know R and just consume

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:37.860
apps and reports.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.200
For these, the R
processes typically run

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:41.670
under a service account.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:43.450
And if you look at
our two products,

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:47.460
you'll see that RStudio Server
Pro is serving R programmers

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:50.030
and RStudio Connect
is serving end users.

00:13:50.030 --> 00:13:55.020
The way you configure R
programmers and end users

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:59.040
are probably going to be
slightly different experiences.

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.860
And those hooking up the systems
will be slightly different

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:03.070
as well.

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:04.470
So how do you hook those up?

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:06.660
Well, that's where
authentication comes in.

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:09.240
Your organization probably
has strong opinions

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:11.640
on how to authenticate users.

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:14.850
This space is only getting
more fragmented, not less.

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:17.670
So if you look at a lot
of on-prem solutions today

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:20.730
will have LDAP,
Active Directory.

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:24.570
And then a lot of the web
technologies, the cloud

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:27.510
technologies, have things
like Octa, Duo, and Auth

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:30.520
and Ping and Shibboleth
and many, many more.

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:33.953
So we want to support as
many systems as possible

00:14:33.953 --> 00:14:35.620
to work with your
authentication system.

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:38.160
And we're working
hard to do that.

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:41.220
And then you will go ahead
and configure RStudio

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:44.040
with whatever
authentication system

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:46.770
that your organization has.

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:49.950
Now, if our products
don't support

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:52.140
your particular
authentication system,

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:54.650
then we recommend
using proxied auth.

00:14:54.650 --> 00:14:57.560
And proxied auth, users
don't log in through RStudio.

00:14:57.560 --> 00:15:00.120
They actually log through
a proxy that you set up.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:02.470
And all of this
is in the guides.

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:04.710
The supported auth methods
for RStudio Server Pro

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:06.290
are PAM pretty much.

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:10.970
And RStudio Connect the
supported methods are typically

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:14.920
LDAP, Active Directory
or PAM and SAML,

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:16.180
which is coming up soon.

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:18.360
So we're working on SAML.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:19.930
Finally, I want to
mention Kerberos,

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:21.180
which isn't an authentication.

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:22.140
It's access control.

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:24.360
But use Kerberos to
connect to databases.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:27.840
And those are supported
by our drivers.

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:30.150
Our professional drivers
allow you to connect

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:31.230
to databases using ODBC.

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:39.450
All right, so in summary
here's my recommendations.

00:15:39.450 --> 00:15:41.120
Number 1, keep your
operating systems

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:42.650
and browsers up to date.

00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:45.530
Number 2, plan to support
multiple versions of R

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:47.180
by building R from source.

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:49.010
Number 3, organize
your R packages

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:51.230
for reliability and consistency.

00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:54.050
Number 4, use root
privileges to install and run

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:54.950
RStudio products.

00:15:54.950 --> 00:16:00.050
And number 5, securely manage
R programmers and end users.

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:02.960
So if you stay to these
five things as you architect

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:08.600
and execute your R platform, you
will have a lot more success.

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:10.370
And there's some information.

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:12.710
So we've written up some
information about how

00:16:12.710 --> 00:16:14.500
to do this online as well.

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:16.130
If you come to
this page, you can

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:19.400
see you've got professional
product requirements.

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:21.290
And I go through all
the professional product

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:22.040
requirements here.

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:32.647
All right, so now that we
have like a path for success,

00:16:32.647 --> 00:16:34.230
what does it look
like to get started?

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:39.080
So the first thing is
to get the right tools.

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:42.075
RStudio makes tools
that you put together.

00:16:42.075 --> 00:16:43.700
They are many ways
to assemble R tools.

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:44.960
But it's going to be
up to you to decide

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:46.332
how to put them together.

00:16:46.332 --> 00:16:48.290
Your configuration depends
on what data science

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:49.850
means to your organization.

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:52.190
Our goal is to make it as
easy to install and configure

00:16:52.190 --> 00:16:54.420
all of our products.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:57.660
So your solutions might
look like a data science lab

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:00.420
where all your data
scientists work together.

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:03.900
Maybe you're building
applications in a dev test prod

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:07.200
framework that you're trying
to orchestrate and deliver

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:08.550
to end users.

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:11.940
You could be on-premises or
on a cloud or a hybrid cloud.

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:14.910
You could have a single
server or multi-departmental

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:18.480
deployments depending on how
your large organization is.

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:20.310
No matter what you're
doing, I typically

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:21.970
recommend thinking
about a crawl,

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:23.640
walk, run strategy,
where you think

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:26.069
about what the platform is
going to look like long term.

00:17:26.069 --> 00:17:27.900
And then you start
with crawl and walk.

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:29.820
I see a lot of
organizations doing that.

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:32.890
And I think that's
a fine strategy.

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:37.310
So this is what a setup
might look like for you.

00:17:37.310 --> 00:17:43.860
RStudio might be for
your development platform

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:46.950
and will connect to your
projects and your home file

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:47.670
shares.

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:50.550
Your RStudio Connect will
be your publishing platform

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:54.920
and will connect to your
file share and your metadata.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:57.090
And then your
Package Manager will

00:17:57.090 --> 00:17:59.190
support both of these products.

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:01.688
And then down below here,
we have some databases,

00:18:01.688 --> 00:18:03.480
R data sources that
you're going to hook up

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:06.940
with our professional drivers.

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:09.100
Now, the number of
servers that you use

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:14.140
and the size of the servers you
use varies across the board.

00:18:14.140 --> 00:18:16.630
You can put all three
products onto the same server.

00:18:16.630 --> 00:18:17.913
I would call that a sandbox.

00:18:17.913 --> 00:18:18.580
And that's fine.

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:20.050
We do that internally.

00:18:20.050 --> 00:18:23.290
You could put a different
product onto each server.

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:24.940
And this is typically
what we recommend

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:27.860
is separate the products
onto their own servers.

00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:30.420
And we call that a
data science lab.

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:33.200
You have three servers
that are working together.

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:35.357
You can double that
for high availability

00:18:35.357 --> 00:18:36.940
if you'd like to
make sure that you're

00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:41.060
going to have a server to fail
over to if there's a problem.

00:18:41.060 --> 00:18:43.230
And then if you want
to extend those out

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:45.770
on a clustered approach,
you could do that as well.

00:18:45.770 --> 00:18:48.050
We have some guidelines
for hardware configuration

00:18:48.050 --> 00:18:49.080
and sizing.

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:51.280
And you can look
at that and kind of

00:18:51.280 --> 00:18:54.040
get started with the
size of your servers.

00:18:57.158 --> 00:18:59.200
All right, so once you
have your hardware set up,

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:03.040
let's talk about recipes
for doing the installation

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:04.060
and configuration.

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:06.280
Recipes are a list
of ingredients

00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:07.450
that make up your platform.

00:19:07.450 --> 00:19:09.820
They help you organize
and automate your work.

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:13.350
And they're probably going to
be unique to your organization.

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:17.560
Now, most recipes
will have a structure

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:21.800
that will look like code for
Linux, R, and R packages.

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:25.360
Then there will be a small
section for your installation.

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:29.150
And then the rest of it will
be about your configuration.

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:31.030
So if you've
installed R properly,

00:19:31.030 --> 00:19:34.700
installation will be
pretty easy to do.

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:39.340
The configuration can
be simple or complex.

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:42.280
So I went back and pulled
out some of my old recipes.

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:44.350
And I wanted to
show you one here.

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:46.150
This is actually
the recipe that I

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:51.760
used to build the box that this
script is actually running on.

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:53.740
And you can see
that most of it is

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:55.570
around Linux and
just getting set up,

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:57.383
getting the dependencies.

00:19:57.383 --> 00:19:59.050
And then I come down
here to the section

00:19:59.050 --> 00:20:04.493
where I show multiple versions
of R being built from source.

00:20:04.493 --> 00:20:06.660
And then here's my packages,
all the packages that I

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:08.190
installed in my system library.

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.797
And then this little
section is the install.

00:20:10.797 --> 00:20:11.880
And that's really typical.

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:14.880
If you installed R properly,
hopefully our installs

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:16.560
go really easily.

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:19.590
And then this last section
is the configuration.

00:20:19.590 --> 00:20:21.470
And like I said,
your configuration

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:22.890
may be complex or simple.

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:25.290
This is a pretty
simple configuration.

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:28.410
I just set a few things around
the groups and the dashboard

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:29.610
and memory limits.

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:32.650
And then that's it.

00:20:32.650 --> 00:20:34.890
So this is a really
simple recipe.

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:37.585
It's something that
you can run by hand.

00:20:37.585 --> 00:20:39.460
What you really want to
do with these recipes

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:42.180
is go ahead and automate them.

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:45.450
So my colleague, Kelly
O'Brien, gave a great talk

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:49.230
at RStudio Conf called
Configuration, Management,

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:53.220
and Tools for the R Admin, where
she argued for infrastructure

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:57.060
as code, using things like
Ansable, Chef, Puppet,

00:20:57.060 --> 00:21:00.910
CodeDeploy, SaltStack,
or systems like these.

00:21:00.910 --> 00:21:02.910
And what these do is they
organize your recipes,

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:05.670
so that you can manage your
platform more effectively

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:07.650
and automatically.

00:21:07.650 --> 00:21:10.080
So I definitely encourage you
to have those conversations

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:12.870
with your team on
how to set that up.

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:15.960
You need to be very good
at deploying R and managing

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:20.700
upgrades and helping
these platforms scale out.

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:25.080
And to do that, you might
want to buy or procure

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:28.380
a sandbox that allows
you to play around

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:30.480
with the configuration scripts.

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:33.370
So the sandboxes are
great places to learn.

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:35.580
And if you haven't
done this before,

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:38.850
it just typically involves
reading a lot of docs

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:40.290
and trying things out.

00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:46.095
It's tax season, and playing
with system administration

00:21:46.095 --> 00:21:47.470
reminds me a little
bit of taxes,

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:50.610
you just have to read a lot
of really boring and detailed

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:53.340
documents and try things out.

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:58.980
Hopefully, when you make
a mistake in your sandbox,

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:00.480
that will be a
lot less forgiving

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:03.600
than the IRS will be if you
make a mistake in your taxes.

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:07.230
But the general idea
is open up the docs,

00:22:07.230 --> 00:22:12.853
get familiar with the
work, and try things out.

00:22:12.853 --> 00:22:14.270
All right, so what
if you've never

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:16.940
used any of these products?

00:22:16.940 --> 00:22:20.480
Well, that's where the
RStudio Quickstart comes in.

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:23.210
So RStudio Quickstart
is a virtual machine

00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:25.340
that runs on your
desktop and includes

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:28.040
all of our professional
products and includes

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:30.410
pre-built assets for you
to explore and demonstrate

00:22:30.410 --> 00:22:31.470
to others.

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:33.830
So the reason we did
this was so that people

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:36.950
could experience RStudio
professional products freely

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:38.330
and easily.

00:22:38.330 --> 00:22:41.170
And I want to go ahead and demo
the Quickstart to you today.

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:46.970
This is the Quickstart page.

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:48.680
This is the location.

00:22:48.680 --> 00:22:50.930
It's fairly easy to find.

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:53.810
You enter your name and email.

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:56.948
And the download will take
about 20 minutes to download.

00:22:56.948 --> 00:22:58.115
It's a fairly large payload.

00:22:58.115 --> 00:23:01.840
If your internet connection
is faster, maybe 10 minutes.

00:23:01.840 --> 00:23:04.720
And then you'll need to
also download and install

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:06.020
virtual box.

00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:12.640
And virtual box allows you to
run this VM on your Windows

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:15.010
or your Mac desktops.

00:23:15.010 --> 00:23:16.480
There's instructions for both.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:21.100
You just pull up the appliance
and hit the Go button,

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:26.110
and you will be good to go.

00:23:26.110 --> 00:23:29.380
Once that's ready to go,
you'll point your browser

00:23:29.380 --> 00:23:31.330
to local host 5000.

00:23:31.330 --> 00:23:33.604
And it will look
something like this.

00:23:33.604 --> 00:23:35.650
Well, it won't look
like that at all.

00:23:35.650 --> 00:23:38.510
It will look like this.

00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:39.230
There you go.

00:23:39.230 --> 00:23:41.600
So this is our Welcome page.

00:23:41.600 --> 00:23:44.270
And we can take a tour.

00:23:44.270 --> 00:23:48.570
And you can find out all
about RStudio Connect

00:23:48.570 --> 00:23:51.420
and play around with that and
a little bit about RStudio

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:52.530
Server Pro as well.

00:23:54.943 --> 00:23:56.360
So let's go ahead
and get started.

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:59.430
We'll just log into
RStudio Connect.

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:02.910
This is the main UI
for RStudio Connect.

00:24:02.910 --> 00:24:06.210
And you can see there's some
assets here that I've already

00:24:06.210 --> 00:24:09.360
put in to the tool.

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:12.300
And I'm going to go ahead and
show you a couple of those.

00:24:14.930 --> 00:24:17.150
This is the Enterprise
Ready Dashboards.

00:24:17.150 --> 00:24:19.160
The great thing
about this dashboard

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:23.397
is that it runs on Shiny
on RStudio Connect.

00:24:23.397 --> 00:24:25.730
And it connects to a database
that we actually installed

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:29.262
inside of the virtual machine.

00:24:29.262 --> 00:24:31.010
And this is done with D3.

00:24:31.010 --> 00:24:35.640
I can click this
and we'll zoom in.

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:38.040
If I drill down, it
will open up a new tab.

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:42.190
And drill down again,
open up another new tab.

00:24:42.190 --> 00:24:47.920
And I can decide who gets
to see this dashboard.

00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:50.020
So for example, right
now, anyone can see it.

00:24:50.020 --> 00:24:52.780
But if I just want to
limit it to specific users,

00:24:52.780 --> 00:24:54.910
I could do that as well.

00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:56.550
I can also decide
how many resources

00:24:56.550 --> 00:24:57.720
to give the dashboard.

00:24:57.720 --> 00:24:58.940
Now, this isn't a VM.

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:02.170
So the resources are
going to be limited.

00:25:02.170 --> 00:25:03.720
But if this were on
a server, I could

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:05.280
increase the number
of connections

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:06.360
and serve more users.

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:11.400
So this is a Shiny application.

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:14.340
And Shiny applications
are really

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:16.950
great for interactive analysis.

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:19.260
Sometimes you need to
run things in batch mode

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:22.830
and send snapshots of the
work that you're doing.

00:25:22.830 --> 00:25:24.450
And for that type
of work, you want

00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:27.670
to use an R Markdown document.

00:25:27.670 --> 00:25:30.090
So here is another dashboard.

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:32.460
But it's written in R Markdown.

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:35.763
So it might look like
a Shiny application.

00:25:35.763 --> 00:25:36.680
But it's actually not.

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:38.100
It's actually a
static dashboard.

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:41.135
It just has a lot of interactive
graphics inside of it.

00:25:41.135 --> 00:25:42.510
And what's cool
about this one is

00:25:42.510 --> 00:25:45.780
that I can actually make
changes to this dashboard

00:25:45.780 --> 00:25:49.450
through parameters that
I've added to my document.

00:25:49.450 --> 00:25:51.750
So R Markdown can
be parametrized.

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:55.050
So here, if I
select new settings

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:59.190
and I do a new window, run
up the acceptance rate,

00:25:59.190 --> 00:26:00.420
I can run this report.

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:04.080
And this will update that
report with those settings.

00:26:04.080 --> 00:26:07.200
When I'm done with that, I
can go ahead and send out

00:26:07.200 --> 00:26:08.910
the email.

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:10.610
So I'll go ahead
and run this report.

00:26:10.610 --> 00:26:12.240
While it's running,
I'll come over here

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:13.660
and look at the email.

00:26:13.660 --> 00:26:17.070
This email is also inside
of the virtual machine.

00:26:17.070 --> 00:26:20.160
And you can see it has a star
because I just sent it today,

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:21.130
just now.

00:26:21.130 --> 00:26:23.980
And this is what the
email looks like.

00:26:23.980 --> 00:26:25.390
It's got some graphics in it.

00:26:25.390 --> 00:26:26.995
It's got some information.

00:26:26.995 --> 00:26:30.100
It tells me like let me know
if you have any questions.

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:33.650
And I've attached my
Excel document here,

00:26:33.650 --> 00:26:38.870
which allows me to pull up
Excel and look at the data.

00:26:38.870 --> 00:26:42.750
These are the data
in this report.

00:26:42.750 --> 00:26:46.740
So this is a great way to
share your information.

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:50.060
And if you want to
distribute your information,

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:52.388
you can do that as
well on a schedule.

00:26:52.388 --> 00:26:56.030
I'll hit the Schedule.

00:26:56.030 --> 00:26:58.450
I can go ahead and set
the schedule and say,

00:26:58.450 --> 00:27:03.160
I want to run this report every
Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday.

00:27:03.160 --> 00:27:03.760
Save that.

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:08.670
And I can send myself
an email when I'm done.

00:27:08.670 --> 00:27:10.140
I can send it to collaborators.

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:12.900
I can decide who else
I want to send it to.

00:27:12.900 --> 00:27:16.050
And that means I can send
one report to one group

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:18.090
and another version
of this report

00:27:18.090 --> 00:27:19.458
to another group on a schedule.

00:27:19.458 --> 00:27:20.750
I don't have to worry about it.

00:27:20.750 --> 00:27:24.000
And if I do any
issues, those are all

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:25.800
going to be tracked
in the logs here.

00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:27.070
And I can see the logs.

00:27:27.070 --> 00:27:28.000
Go back to my code.

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:32.460
I can fix the code and
publish that information here.

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:36.810
So how do I publish
information back in here.

00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:39.410
Let's take a quick look.

00:27:39.410 --> 00:27:43.390
I'm back to the welcome page.

00:27:43.390 --> 00:27:46.210
And notice I have a lot
of other products in here.

00:27:46.210 --> 00:27:49.142
I've got RStudio Server
Pro in here as well.

00:27:49.142 --> 00:27:50.350
I'll go ahead and click that.

00:27:53.310 --> 00:27:59.430
And this will deliver an
instance of RStudio Pro to me.

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:03.270
And I have a simple
Shiny application here.

00:28:03.270 --> 00:28:05.220
I can run this
application in my IDE.

00:28:09.320 --> 00:28:12.760
And you can see that I
can play around with this.

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:17.615
And I can publish this
to RStudio Connect.

00:28:17.615 --> 00:28:18.810
Oh, it doesn't like it.

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:20.980
Let me go ahead and
create a new one.

00:28:20.980 --> 00:28:28.035
File, New File, Shiny
web app, New app.

00:28:28.035 --> 00:28:29.220
I'll run that.

00:28:33.298 --> 00:28:37.195
I'll publish this
to RStudio Connect.

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:43.430
And while it's publishing,
what it does is it goes back

00:28:43.430 --> 00:28:46.760
and it kind of describes
all the environment

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:49.390
and then sends that information
over to RStudio Connect so it

00:28:49.390 --> 00:28:51.230
can rebuild the
environment along with all

00:28:51.230 --> 00:28:52.655
the code and data assets.

00:28:57.100 --> 00:29:00.060
And there we have it
in RStudio Connect.

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:03.080
And again, I can decide who
I want to share this with.

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:10.140
I can even open this
in its own window.

00:29:10.140 --> 00:29:14.030
And then that URL will
work with anyone that's

00:29:14.030 --> 00:29:15.350
on my machine, which is me.

00:29:15.350 --> 00:29:17.450
I'm the only one on my machine.

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:20.180
But this is how you would
share a Shiny application

00:29:20.180 --> 00:29:22.490
on a server.

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:25.040
Now, in addition to the Shiny
applications and R Markdown

00:29:25.040 --> 00:29:30.260
documents that you can publish,
you can also publish APIs.

00:29:30.260 --> 00:29:35.600
And APIs are basically systems
for allowing one computer

00:29:35.600 --> 00:29:37.280
to talk to another computer.

00:29:37.280 --> 00:29:40.040
And in this case this is
a plumber API and that

00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:44.300
means that it uses REST.

00:29:44.300 --> 00:29:48.590
And that means I can call my
API through a REST based call

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:50.480
like through a browser.

00:29:50.480 --> 00:29:53.090
So I will show you
what this looks like.

00:29:53.090 --> 00:29:55.910
If I go ahead and
try out this API,

00:29:55.910 --> 00:30:04.360
and I say give me the
stock ticker for Facebook,

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:06.670
it gives me all the
information for Facebook.

00:30:06.670 --> 00:30:15.020
And it gives me this URL that
I can use to query the API.

00:30:18.730 --> 00:30:19.800
So there's Facebook.

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:22.670
I can do Microsoft.

00:30:22.670 --> 00:30:24.420
API, pull up Microsoft.

00:30:24.420 --> 00:30:27.360
Now, APIs are extremely helpful
for the handoff problem.

00:30:27.360 --> 00:30:30.960
If you need to hand your R
analysis to someone else,

00:30:30.960 --> 00:30:34.350
then APIs are a great way
to do that, because all you

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:38.280
have to specify to the other
group or the other system

00:30:38.280 --> 00:30:39.570
is the API itself.

00:30:39.570 --> 00:30:41.820
You don't have to
say anything about R.

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:44.610
So you can keep are
running on RStudio Connect

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:47.385
and use that to serve
information to other systems.

00:30:47.385 --> 00:30:48.300
It's really nice.

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:49.502
They're easy to code.

00:30:49.502 --> 00:30:50.460
They're easy to deploy.

