00:36:41.530 --> 00:36:43.690
would be done as AWS.

00:36:43.690 --> 00:36:48.170
How does storage work
between local and Kubernetes?

00:36:48.170 --> 00:36:52.240
So we're going to do another
webinar about deployments

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:55.140
and how to help
deployments work at RStudio

00:36:55.140 --> 00:36:58.720
and what the best strategies
are for doing deployments.

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:02.530
Right now your deployments
are being handled kind

00:37:02.530 --> 00:37:05.923
of on the back end for you.

00:37:05.923 --> 00:37:07.840
You know, you're not
seeing a lot of that work

00:37:07.840 --> 00:37:10.215
like when you push a button
and you deploy an application

00:37:10.215 --> 00:37:12.820
or when you start up a
container and it just

00:37:12.820 --> 00:37:15.520
automatically runs for, you not
seeing the deployment process.

00:37:15.520 --> 00:37:18.340
But what's happening there
is the same thing every time.

00:37:18.340 --> 00:37:20.200
You create an environment.

00:37:20.200 --> 00:37:23.650
You describe the environment
that you're developing.

00:37:23.650 --> 00:37:24.910
You bundle up the code.

00:37:24.910 --> 00:37:25.630
You ship it over.

00:37:25.630 --> 00:37:29.065
And then you reconstitute that
environment in a new location.

00:37:29.065 --> 00:37:30.440
That's just how
deployments work.

00:37:30.440 --> 00:37:31.950
So those three
processes are going

00:37:31.950 --> 00:37:35.830
to carry over every time
you're creating a container.

00:37:35.830 --> 00:37:37.780
So the question is, how
does day storage work?

00:37:37.780 --> 00:37:41.950
Well, you can like
try to ship over

00:37:41.950 --> 00:37:44.142
that information through
environment variables

00:37:44.142 --> 00:37:45.100
or something like that.

00:37:45.100 --> 00:37:49.180
Or you can reattach that
information through shared file

00:37:49.180 --> 00:37:53.790
storages, so shared
file storage.

00:37:53.790 --> 00:38:00.500
Can RStudio Server Pro
run Docker R instances?

00:38:00.500 --> 00:38:07.580
So RStudio Server Pro today
does not run Docker R instances.

00:38:07.580 --> 00:38:09.050
That's the short answer.

00:38:09.050 --> 00:38:12.080
In the future, we'll
have the ability

00:38:12.080 --> 00:38:19.500
to launch jobs using
different container systems.

00:38:19.500 --> 00:38:22.490
Let's see, and again, I
would encourage you go back

00:38:22.490 --> 00:38:24.500
to the blog, that
was a blog post that

00:38:24.500 --> 00:38:27.590
was written last week
that will give you

00:38:27.590 --> 00:38:30.320
more information
about how that works.

00:38:30.320 --> 00:38:34.900
Does the Plumber API
need end users to have R?

00:38:37.690 --> 00:38:38.720
Yeah, so, no.

00:38:38.720 --> 00:38:40.580
No, so the end users
on a Plumber API

00:38:40.580 --> 00:38:43.670
are just making a web
call, just like you're

00:38:43.670 --> 00:38:45.290
calling out to a web page.

00:38:45.290 --> 00:38:47.990
That's the beauty of the APIs
that the R process is actually

00:38:47.990 --> 00:38:49.730
hosted on the RStudio Connect.

00:38:49.730 --> 00:38:51.200
So say you have a
scoring equation

00:38:51.200 --> 00:38:54.380
that you want to run
against like a database,

00:38:54.380 --> 00:38:56.720
your database could actually
be configured to call out

00:38:56.720 --> 00:39:00.080
to RStudio Connect, send it
the information that needs

00:39:00.080 --> 00:39:04.160
to score that model, and
R would run on a connect

00:39:04.160 --> 00:39:08.270
and deliver the score based on
whatever complex analytic model

00:39:08.270 --> 00:39:10.040
that you've created
in R and then send it

00:39:10.040 --> 00:39:12.140
back to that system
without that system ever

00:39:12.140 --> 00:39:16.820
having to use R. That's
the beauty of the APIs.

