00:39:41.310 --> 00:39:43.310
There's the push button
model that I showed you.

00:39:43.310 --> 00:39:46.220
But there's also these
programmatic deployment

00:39:46.220 --> 00:39:54.230
functions that allow you to
publish your work on systems

00:39:54.230 --> 00:39:57.620
that don't have any
information about R.

00:39:57.620 --> 00:40:00.050
And that's useful for
publishing from GitHub,

00:40:00.050 --> 00:40:03.590
for example, or from some
other automated system

00:40:03.590 --> 00:40:05.357
that your organization uses.

00:40:05.357 --> 00:40:06.440
Those are all really good.

00:40:06.440 --> 00:40:09.520
And there's information
about that on Solutions.

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:13.727
So if I come back
here to Solutions,

00:40:13.727 --> 00:40:16.310
and you look at Deployments, you
can find out more information

00:40:16.310 --> 00:40:17.268
about deployments here.

00:40:20.190 --> 00:40:23.700
Where Linux systems have shared
home directories for users,

00:40:23.700 --> 00:40:26.910
what are the best picks for
running multiple RStudio Pro

00:40:26.910 --> 00:40:28.050
servers?

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:32.710
So that's exactly right.

00:40:32.710 --> 00:40:36.750
You're going to want
to mount your shared

00:40:36.750 --> 00:40:40.020
NFS across all of your servers.

00:40:40.020 --> 00:40:41.130
And there's two locations.

00:40:41.130 --> 00:40:44.400
I showed it briefly, but
I'll go into more depth.

00:40:44.400 --> 00:40:46.872
One is for your project files.

00:40:46.872 --> 00:40:48.330
You'll want to put
all your product

00:40:48.330 --> 00:40:50.330
files on the NFS server.

00:40:50.330 --> 00:40:52.450
And the other one is
for your home directory,

00:40:52.450 --> 00:40:55.720
which has some configuration
knobs inside of it for RStudio

00:40:55.720 --> 00:40:56.340
Server Pro.

00:40:56.340 --> 00:41:00.160
Both of those should be mounted
across all of your servers.

00:41:00.160 --> 00:41:04.380
You can think of your RStudio
servers as compute engines

00:41:04.380 --> 00:41:05.420
mostly.

00:41:05.420 --> 00:41:07.098
They ingest
information into RAM.

00:41:07.098 --> 00:41:07.890
They operate on it.

00:41:07.890 --> 00:41:09.600
And they spit things back.

00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:11.742
They don't contain
data long term.

00:41:11.742 --> 00:41:13.200
And that goes with
Connect as well.

00:41:13.200 --> 00:41:15.390
Connect is more of
a compute engine.

00:41:15.390 --> 00:41:18.590
All of your content should
be on a shared file system.

00:41:18.590 --> 00:41:21.580
All of your metadata should
be stored somewhere else.

00:41:21.580 --> 00:41:24.214
So it's not on
those compute nodes.

00:41:24.214 --> 00:41:27.950
I hope that made sense.

00:41:27.950 --> 00:41:31.080
Let's see, what else do we have?

00:41:31.080 --> 00:41:35.445
Are you able to install an
older version of packages?

00:41:38.330 --> 00:41:40.080
I think that's a Package
Manager question.

00:41:40.080 --> 00:41:43.860
And, yes, you can
organize those packages

00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:45.790
any way that you would like.

00:41:45.790 --> 00:41:47.490
Can you explain
air-gapped environments

00:41:47.490 --> 00:41:48.660
for Package Manager?

00:41:48.660 --> 00:41:52.020
So Emilio, I take it that you
haven't had to deal with that,

00:41:52.020 --> 00:41:56.730
because if you did, that
would be a painful question.

00:41:56.730 --> 00:42:00.450
Air-gapped environments are
setup for security purposes.

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:03.000
And those servers don't have
any connection to the internet

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:03.750
at all.

00:42:03.750 --> 00:42:06.840
So the only way you can
get information into those

00:42:06.840 --> 00:42:10.620
is through another process, like
some sort of file copy process

00:42:10.620 --> 00:42:12.210
that the admins control.

00:42:12.210 --> 00:42:13.650
So an air-gapped
environment means

00:42:13.650 --> 00:42:16.320
that if you want to get
the latest information,

00:42:16.320 --> 00:42:17.760
you can't download it.

00:42:17.760 --> 00:42:19.260
You have to go talk
to somebody else

00:42:19.260 --> 00:42:21.915
to have it transferred
over into your setup.

00:42:21.915 --> 00:42:27.090
If you ever had a old
Macintosh circa 1985,

00:42:27.090 --> 00:42:30.912
that would be an
air-gapped environment.

00:42:30.912 --> 00:42:32.870
Could you comment on why
building R from source

00:42:32.870 --> 00:42:35.990
is better than say installing it
from Debian or Ubuntu packages?

00:42:35.990 --> 00:42:38.720
Yeah, so building R
from source is better

00:42:38.720 --> 00:42:44.340
because it builds
against your own system.

00:42:44.340 --> 00:42:46.010
So you're going to
be confident that it

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:47.730
will run with your system.

00:42:47.730 --> 00:42:51.990
So when you compile, it's using
the libraries on your system.

00:42:51.990 --> 00:42:57.530
It also, like I said, allows
you to put R side by side

00:42:57.530 --> 00:43:01.160
with other installations of R.
So it becomes extremely useful.

00:43:01.160 --> 00:43:03.020
But I think best
practices in general

00:43:03.020 --> 00:43:05.570
involve compiling that
software on your system.

00:43:09.180 --> 00:43:11.000
What we tend to use
a repositories for

00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:13.200
is identifying the dependencies.

00:43:13.200 --> 00:43:15.920
So when you build R, you
have to go out and find

00:43:15.920 --> 00:43:19.520
all the things that
are needed to build R.

00:43:19.520 --> 00:43:23.670
And that can be useful.

00:43:23.670 --> 00:43:26.420
The repositories can be
very, very useful for that.

00:43:26.420 --> 00:43:28.930
But actually getting the
binaries from the repositories,

00:43:28.930 --> 00:43:32.030
I try to steer clear from that.

00:43:32.030 --> 00:43:34.490
All right, so any
other things here?

00:43:34.490 --> 00:43:39.010
System-wide libraries versus
user installed-- yeah,

00:43:39.010 --> 00:43:41.378
so, Matt, that
information is coming.

00:43:41.378 --> 00:43:42.920
Like there's a lot
of work that we're

00:43:42.920 --> 00:43:46.670
doing right now when it comes
to a library and package library

00:43:46.670 --> 00:43:49.190
management, making
things reproducible.

00:43:49.190 --> 00:43:51.020
So your question is
a really good one.

00:43:51.020 --> 00:43:54.710
How do you handle libraries,
system-wide libraries

00:43:54.710 --> 00:43:56.240
versus user-installed libraries?

00:43:56.240 --> 00:44:01.400
Traditionally, the sys admin has
control of your system library.

00:44:01.400 --> 00:44:05.780
And then your users have
control of your user libraries.

00:44:05.780 --> 00:44:10.400
And that tends to be
what we recommend today.

00:44:10.400 --> 00:44:13.562
That's kind of the
de facto standard.

00:44:13.562 --> 00:44:15.770
I think in the future, we'll
have better organization

00:44:15.770 --> 00:44:19.130
around libraries and how
they interact with projects

00:44:19.130 --> 00:44:23.200
so that things become
more reproducible.

